 - A backend that supports neither vectored host reads nor staged host-to-device reads (for example kvikIO) is never given a prefetching cache and consumes prepared slices eagerly through the same hook.
 
+**Deferred, vectored device reads.** With a prefetching cache and `deferred_device_reads` enabled, `sirius_datasource::device_read_async` clamps the range, captures the current CUDA device, and collects explicit-stream reads in one batch per datasource, stream and device. It constructs the returned future before publishing the destination. The first `get()`/`wait()` on any member issues the collected ranges as one `device_read_ranges_async`, deferring cache lookup, chunk pins, copies and backend I/O until that wait. Each member resolves from the shared completion to its own byte count. Issuance restores the submission device temporarily, so moving a future to another thread preserves its device and explicit stream.
+
+When several files have queued reads before the first wait, `deferred_batch_registry` lets that waiter issue all their batches on the same stream. With files A, B and C and a first wait on A, the current order is B, C, A. B/C can then make progress while A's issuance waits for an already-running prefetch. This is a heuristic: a blocked B can delay A, and finite backend capacity can favor a different order. The wait still waits for sibling issue calls to return, so A's I/O may finish earlier than A's future wait returns. The [ordering benchmark](../../bench/deferred-device-read-order/README.md) compares these cases. Registry entries are weak and each issue takes only its own batch mutex. Null, legacy-default and per-thread-default streams issue eagerly on the submitting thread because their identity cannot safely be moved between threads or devices.
+
+`wait_for`/`wait_until` report `future_status::deferred` without issuing anything. A batch whose futures are all dropped before issuance expires. Once accepted, each destination must remain valid until its batch completes, even if its own future is dropped: a surviving member or another datasource's waiter may issue it. Destruction cancels unissued batches without allocation; later `get()` reports `std::logic_error`, or `std::bad_alloc` if constructing the cancellation error fails. Issue errors are retained for every member without retrying. Each issue increments `deferred_flushes` and logs its ranges/bytes at debug level. `scan_manager.cache.deferred_device_reads: false` issues reads eagerly. Explicit vectored reads and host reads retain their existing behavior.
+
 The ioctx is built parked: the reactor pool is constructed cheaply at `make_ioctx` time, and `start()` launches the worker threads and allocates per-reactor staging only once the read API is first exercised.
 
